  "experiment": "E7",
  "hidden_pattern": "Failsafe bias is usually checked against the two 120 Ohm terminators alone, giving a 60 Ohm bus. The 32-unit-load receiver input resistance (about 375 Ohm) sits in parallel with that, lowering the bus to 51.7 Ohm and the bias voltage by about 14%. A 680 Ohm bias that passes 5 V unloaded (211 mV) fails with full load (183 mV); 3.3 V needs about 390 Ohm, not 430 Ohm. Separately, dropping DE at TXE rather than TC truncates the final byte: the shift register can still be sending 11 bit times of data.",
